<!-- Post title -->
<h3 class="storytitle" id="post-28"><a href="http://www.marksportal.net/blog/index.php?p=28" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Self-Esteem">Self-Esteem</a></h3>
<!-- Post meta -->
<div class="meta">
Posted on July 7th, 2004. <!-- By Mark. --> About <a href="http://www.marksportal.net/blog/index.php?cat=1" title="View all posts in General">General</a>. <a href="http://www.marksportal.net/blog/index.php?p=28#comments">No Comments</a>.</div>

<!-- Actual post -->
<div class="storycontent">
	<p>The last few weeks have been a kind of emotional roller coaster ride for me.  It&#8217;s quite irritating actually.  As much as I try not to, I find that I place a lot of self-worth in how others view me.   As a friend put it, &#8220;Unless otherwise stated by people, you assume that you are disliked&#8221;.  This is a very true statement.  For a majority of my life I&#8217;ve felt like I really don&#8217;t have that much to offer others and as such, am not valued.   I remember in high school I was walking down the hallway and one of the popular H.S guys said, &#8220;Hi Mark&#8221; when we passed each other.   I replied back, but inside I was shocked.  He knew my name!   I don&#8217;t know why it surprised me so much.  The school wasn&#8217;t that big and I had been in classes with him throughout the years, but it still surprised me&#8230;and made me feel good.   I find the same thoughts/emotions happen to me today when someone comes up and says they were talking about me or thinking about me sometime when I wasn&#8217;t around.  People thinking about me when I&#8217;m not in front of them?!  For some reason I figure if I&#8217;m out of sight, I&#8217;m out of mind.  I know it&#8217;s a childish thought, but nonetheless, that&#8217;s what I think.  So, for the last several weeks I&#8217;ve been struggling with a self-esteem issue, which has dipped down below my normal low levels.  A part of this I chalk up to not spending enough time with God and the other part I chalk up to spending too much time thinking about what I imagine someone else&#8217;s opinion of me is.  So last night I spent some time with God and went to bed a bit earlier than normal.  I find that if I&#8217;m busy focusing on listening and talking to God, my mind doesn&#8217;t have time to focus on what I think others think about me.</p>
	<p>I&#8217;ve tried to be as honest as possible in my posts.   That being the case, you might ask yourself how I can place my self-worth in others and yet be so un-guarded here.  It&#8217;s easy.  First, my posts go out to an empty void as far as I&#8217;m concerned.  I would be very surprised if someone came back to me and told me they actually read my entries.   Secondly, everybody has some sort of fault, weakness, struggle or scar from life.  By sharing my thoughts/struggles I&#8217;m not introducing anything new to the world.  I&#8217;m just giving people a view of the things from another set of eyes.  </p>
	<p>Well my time is up.  I had wanted to talk about faith for a bit, but that will have to wait for another time.
</p>
</div>			
